Abstract

The transition from software agents to cyber-physical agents changes the mathematical structure of autonomous decision-making. A software agent manipulates information; a cyber-physical agent additionally modifies physical state through actuation, consuming energy and material resources, altering equipment condition, and generating consequences that may be computationally irreversible. This work presents a complete theoretical framework for Physical Agency Constraints (PAC): a formal approach to bounding the effective action space of autonomous agents operating cyber-physical systems. The framework incorporates a threat model distinguishing random failure, adversarial attack, and specification gaming; a hierarchical feasibility structure with a formally proven Feasibility Lemma guaranteeing solution existence at every time step; a Delayed Safety Proposition linking temporal validity to reachable-set containment; a corrected Monotonicity Theorem restricted to monotone systems via dominance functions and a Design Axiom for general systems; a resource-aware viability kernel making irreversibility a formal exclusion rather than a philosophical claim; a certified approximate reachability grounding via CARe; three admissible perception contracts (P1)–(P3) through which any perception stack must interact with the safety authority; and a precise mapping to functional safety standards (IEC 61508, ISO 26262, ISO/IEC TR 5469, ISO/PAS 8800). The central principle is that an AI agent should not possess unrestricted physical actuation authority: its admissible action space must be continuously constrained by physical safety invariants, state-estimation uncertainty, model uncertainty, temporal validity, resource limitations, and independently enforced fail-safe mechanisms.

