1. Introduction

1.1 The Structural Transition

Modern AI agents increasingly move beyond purely informational tasks. An agent may now write and execute software, interact with external APIs, operate robotic systems, control industrial processes, and optimize energy production. This transition changes the consequences of action.

In a software environment, many experiments can be copied, sandboxed, replayed, reverted, or terminated at relatively low marginal cost. In a physical environment, an action may consume energy, transform material, accelerate equipment wear, change thermal or mechanical state, reduce future operational options, damage equipment, or expose humans to risk. The deeper transition is therefore not merely \text{software} \rightarrow \text{hardware} but

\boxed{\text{information manipulation} \rightarrow \text{causal physical intervention}.}

The agent is no longer merely producing an output; it is changing the state of the world in which its subsequent decisions will occur. This structural change requires a corresponding change in the mathematical apparatus used to reason about the agent's authority.

1.2 Contributions

This monograph presents a complete theoretical framework for Physical Agency Constraints (PAC). The contributions are:

1. A formal notation ledger fixing the containment chain \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} \subseteq \mathcal{U} and eliminating the conflation of action proposals with authority sets (Section 4.0).
2. A three-class threat model distinguishing random failure and distribution shift, adversarial attack on perception and actuation, and specification gaming (Section 3).
3. A Feasibility Lemma (Lemma 1) with proof, guaranteeing that a feasible action exists at every time step under the hierarchical structure (Section 6.5).
4. A Delayed Safety Proposition (Proposition 1) linking the temporal-validity condition \tau < T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) to reachable-set containment, with the control barrier functional framework providing the correct treatment of input delay (Section 4.5).
5. A corrected Monotonicity Theorem (Theorem 1) for monotone systems via dominance functions, and Design Axiom D1 for general systems (Section 10).
6. A resource-aware viability kernel \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}} integrating energy, material, and wear state into the safety authority's computation (Section 9).
7. A certified approximate reachability grounding via CARe, with honest treatment of the resource-augmented tractability gap (Section 7).
8. Three admissible perception contracts (P1)–(P3) through which any perception stack—including learned modules—must interact with the safety authority (Section 8.4).
