2. Formal Preliminaries

2.1 Plant Model

Let the physical system be represented by

x_{t+1} = F(x_t, u_t, w_t), \qquad x_t \in \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n,\; u_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m,\; w_t \in \mathcal{W},

where x_t is the physical state, u_t is the applied control, and w_t represents disturbances and model uncertainty. For continuous-time formulations we write

\dot{x} = f(x) + g(x)u + d, \qquad d \in \mathcal{D},

with f and g locally Lipschitz and \mathcal{D} a bounded disturbance set. The flow map over an interval [0, T] is denoted \phi_T(x_0, u(\cdot), d(\cdot)).

2.2 Observations and Belief

The physical state is not directly observable. Observations satisfy

y_t = H(x_t, v_t),

with v_t measurement noise. The agent operates on a belief

b_t(x) = \mathbb{P}(x_t = x \mid y_{0:t}, u_{0:t-1}),

and a generic estimator provides

(\hat{x}_t, \Sigma_t) = \mathcal{E}(y_{0:t}, u_{0:t-1}),

where \hat{x}_t is the estimated state and \Sigma_t is an uncertainty covariance or set-membership bound. The architectural principle is

\boxed{\text{Perception} \rightarrow \text{State Estimation} \rightarrow \text{Safety Decision}.}

The AI should not directly interpret raw physical observations as unrestricted control authority.

2.3 Safety Region and Forward Invariance

Let

\mathcal{C} = \{x : h(x) \geq 0\}

represent a physically safe region, with h : \mathcal{X} \to \mathbb{R} continuously differentiable. The safe set \mathcal{C} is forward-invariant under a controller \pi if for all x_0 \in \mathcal{C} and all admissible disturbances, the closed-loop trajectory satisfies x(t) \in \mathcal{C} for all t \geq 0.

For control-affine systems, a control barrier function (CBF) condition sufficient for forward invariance is

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ \nabla h(x)^\top (f(x) + g(x)u) \right] \geq -\alpha(h(x)),

where \alpha is a class-\mathcal{K} function. Under appropriate regularity and feasibility assumptions, this condition establishes \mathcal{C} as forward-invariant.

2.4 Viability Kernel

The viability kernel is the largest safe set from which the system can be kept viable indefinitely:

\mathcal{K} = \left\{ x : \exists \pi \; \forall d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}, \; x(t) \in \mathcal{C} \; \forall t \geq 0 \right\}.

CBFs are developed upon viability theory and can be used to maintain all states within the viability kernel. Monotonicity properties of both dynamics and constraints allow for analytical upper and lower approximations of the viability kernel through weakly and strongly invariant sets.

