3. Threat Model

The safety authority must be designed against a specified threat model. We distinguish three threat classes, each requiring different architectural provisions.

3.1 Random Failure and Distribution Shift

Deep learning models produce imperfect predictions due to distribution shift between training and deployment, sensor degradation, and stochastic environmental variation. The Simplex architecture addresses this threat through a safety monitor that oversees the state of the system and switches to a simpler but safer backup module whenever the advanced controller's behavior is considered untrustworthy.

3.2 Adversarial Attack on Perception and Actuation

CPS attack surfaces include sensor spoofing, actuation tampering, denial-of-service, and privilege elevation. A safety monitor designed for bounded disturbances is not robust to a spoofed sensor. The Simplex architecture explicitly addresses security by isolating the neural network in a rich operating system deemed untrustworthy, while safety-critical functions run in a separate real-time domain under a type-1 hypervisor.

3.3 Specification Gaming

The AI may satisfy its formal objective while violating the spirit of safety constraints. This is the most difficult threat class because the agent is not merely unreliable but potentially adversarial with respect to the safety specification. No purely architectural solution exists; the relevant defenses are specification robustness, runtime monitoring, and human governance.

3.4 Conjunction of Independent Safety Properties

Let \mathcal{S} = \{S_1, \ldots, S_k\} be a set of safety properties, each expressible as \mathcal{C}_i \subseteq \mathcal{X}. The safety authority enforces the conjunction

\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{total}} = \bigcap_{i=1}^{k} \mathcal{C}_i.

Formal condition (non-expressibility). There exists i^\star \in \{1, \ldots, k\} such that for all reward functions J' equivalent to the agent's objective J under positive affine transformation,

\{ x \in \mathcal{X} : x \in \mathcal{C}_{i^\star} \} \not\equiv \{ x \in \mathcal{X} : J'(x) \geq c \} \quad \text{for any } c \in \mathbb{R}.

Equivalently: S_{i^\star} is not a superlevel set of any reward-equivalent objective.

Enforcement. The property S_{i^\star} is implemented in Layer 1 (formally verified inner layer) of the layered assurance architecture (Section 12.2), not in the AI's reward function. Layer 1 consists of hardware interlocks, PLC safety systems, and certified firmware that are independent of the AI module and cannot be modified by it. The AI's action u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} is passed through the Layer 1 enforcement as a final check before actuation:

u_t = \Pi_{\mathrm{Layer 1}}\!\left(\Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}\!\left(u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}\right)\right),

where \Pi_{\mathrm{Layer 1}} projects onto the set of actions satisfying S_{i^\star} (and any other Layer 1 properties). If \Pi_{\mathrm{Layer 1}} rejects the action, the system transitions to Level 1 (recovery) or Level 2 (fail-safe).

Architectural consequence. Because S_{i^\star} is not expressible in J, the agent cannot optimize it directly. Because it is enforced in Layer 1, the agent cannot bypass it. This raises the bar for specification gaming without claiming to eliminate it.

Honest limitation. A sufficiently capable agent may still find a policy that satisfies all \mathcal{C}_i while violating their spirit. The conjunction is a necessary but not sufficient condition.

