4. Physical Agency Constraints: Core Framework

4.0 Notation Ledger

The framework uses the following notation consistently throughout. This ledger is the single source of truth for all containment relations.

Symbol Meaning Enforced by
\mathcal{U} Full actuation space Abstract
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} Physically realizable actions (hard actuator limits, PLC interlocks) Hardware / certified firmware
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) Safety-admissible actions given state, uncertainty, delay, resources Safety authority
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{AI}} Actions proposed by the AI policy \pi AI module
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{applied}} Actions actually applied to the plant Projection \Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}
\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} The envelope from which AI proposals may be drawn \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} \cap \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}}

The containment chain is

\boxed{u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}}

with the safety projection

u_t = \Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}\!\left(u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}\right) \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}}.

Important distinction. The AI's proposal u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{AI}} is an element of the actuation space, not a subset of it; the authority envelope \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} is the set from which proposals may be drawn. This distinction eliminates the conflation present in earlier formulations.

4.1 The Authority Gap

The agent proposes

u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} = \pi(o_{0:t}, m_t),

but the action actually applied to the plant satisfies u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}. Therefore

\boxed{u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} \neq u_t \quad \text{in general}}

because the proposed action may be rejected, modified, delayed, or replaced by a safety controller. The effective authority of the AI is \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}.

4.2 Capability vs. Authority

A central hypothesis is

\boxed{\text{Capability} \neq \text{Authority}.}

An AI model may be capable of generating an action without possessing the authority to execute it. Define the state-dependent admissible action set

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}_t, \Sigma_t, \tau_t, E_t, M_t),

where \Sigma_t is state uncertainty, \tau_t is decision/verification delay, E_t is available energy budget, and M_t is safety margin.

4.3 Temporal Validity

Physical actions are executed under latency:

\tau = \tau_{\mathrm{sensing}} + \tau_{\mathrm{computation}} + \tau_{\mathrm{communication}} + \tau_{\mathrm{actuation}} + \tau_{\mathrm{verification}}.

Define the safety horizon T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) as the remaining time before the system reaches a critical boundary under the relevant dynamics. A necessary architectural condition for an action requiring intervention before that boundary is

\boxed{\tau < T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x).}

This distinguishes correctness from temporal validity. The condition is necessary but not sufficient: worst-case execution time distributions and jitter must also be bounded.

4.4 Resource State and Irreversibility

A physical agent consumes resources. Let

r_t = (E_t, M_t, W_t, \ldots)

be the resource state, where E_t is available energy/exergy, M_t is available material, and W_t is equipment condition or wear state. Define the augmented state

\tilde{x}_t = (x_t, r_t) \in \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{R},

with resource dynamics

r_{t+1} = R(x_t, r_t, u_t),

where R is monotone in u_t in the sense that larger actuation magnitudes deplete E_t and M_t faster and increase wear W_t. The admissible resource set is

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} = \left\{ r : E \geq E_{\min},\; M \geq M_{\min},\; W \leq W_{\max} \right\}.

The transition is (x_t, r_t) \rightarrow (x_{t+1}, r_{t+1}). An action may reduce future capability: \boxed{a_t \rightarrow \mathcal{A}_{t+1}} where \mathcal{A}_{t+1} is the future feasible action space. This is a key difference between physical agency and purely computational interaction: the agent can consume part of its own future operational freedom.

4.5 Delayed Safety

The temporal-validity condition \tau < T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) is necessary but not sufficient. The following proposition links it to reachable-set containment.

Proposition 1 (Delayed Safety via Reachable Set Containment). Let the system be subject to a sensing and computation delay \tau = \tau_{\mathrm{sensing}} + \tau_{\mathrm{computation}}. The state estimate \hat{x}(t - \tau) is used to compute the safety-admissible action at time t. Define the delay-reachable set

\mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\hat{x}) = \left\{ x' : \exists d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}, \; x' = \phi_{\tau}(\hat{x}, d) \right\},

where \phi_{\tau} is the flow map over the delay interval. Then forward invariance of \mathcal{C} under the delayed feedback policy holds if and only if

\boxed{\mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\hat{x}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}.}

Proof sketch. Necessity: if \mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\hat{x}) \not\subseteq \mathcal{C}, there exists a disturbance realization driving the state outside \mathcal{C} before the delayed action takes effect. Sufficiency: if the containment holds, then for all disturbance realizations, the state remains in \mathcal{C} throughout the delay interval, and the delayed action computed from \hat{x}(t-\tau) is admissible at the time of application because the true state is still in \mathcal{C}. ∎

Connection to (P1). Under the set-membership contract (P1), the estimator provides \mathcal{X}_t \ni x_t. The delay-reachable set is then \mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\mathcal{X}_{t-\tau}) = \bigcup_{\hat{x} \in \mathcal{X}_{t-\tau}} \mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\hat{x}), and the safety condition becomes \mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(\mathcal{X}_{t-\tau}) \subseteq \mathcal{C}. This makes the temporal-validity condition \tau < T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) a consequence of the reachable-set containment: the safety horizon T_{\mathrm{safe}}(x) is the largest \tau for which \mathcal{R}_{[0,\tau]}(x) \subseteq \mathcal{C}.

Remark on input delay. The proposition is stated for state delay (where the measurement is delayed but the actuation is instantaneous). For input delay (where the actuation itself is delayed), the analysis is more involved because the relative degree is affected by the delay itself. The correct machinery is the theory of control barrier functionals, which extends CBFs to systems with state delay by introducing functionals that enforce forward invariance of safe sets defined in the infinite-dimensional state space. Control barrier functionals guide the selection of safe control inputs for time-delay systems in a similar fashion to how CBFs yield safety for delay-free systems. The framework should state that input delay requires the control barrier functional machinery, not the simple reachable-set containment.

