5. Safety Authority Architecture

5.1 Architectural Principle

The AI agent should not be the sole authority determining whether its own actions are safe. The architecture is

\boxed{\text{AI Agent} \rightarrow \text{Safety Authority} \rightarrow \text{Controller} \rightarrow \text{Plant}.}

The safety authority may combine CBFs, model predictive control, reachability analysis, runtime monitoring, invariant checking, hard actuator limits, PLC safety systems, and emergency-stop mechanisms. The final physical actuation must satisfy independently enforced constraints:

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} = \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} \cap \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}}, \qquad u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} \subseteq \mathcal{U}.

This is the single containment chain used throughout the paper; all other statements are equivalent restatements. The AI's proposal u_t^{\mathrm{AI}} \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{AI}} is an element of the actuation space, not a subset of it; the authority envelope \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} is the set from which proposals may be drawn.

5.2 Relationship to Simplex Architecture

The Simplex architecture is the canonical implementation of this structure. An unverified advanced controller proposes actions; a verified baseline controller can take over when the advanced controller violates a safety property. The Simplex architecture enhances safety by leveraging two isolated execution domains: one dedicated to the execution of neural networks under a rich operating system deemed not trustworthy, and one responsible for running safety-critical functions under a real-time operating system, with both domains hosted on the same platform and isolated through a type-1 real-time hypervisor.

PAC extends Simplex in two directions:

1. Dynamic authority computation. Rather than a binary switch between advanced and baseline controllers, PAC computes a continuous authority bound \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}}(\hat{x}_t, \Sigma_t, \tau_t, E_t) that modulates the agent's permitted action space.
2. Uncertainty-aware authority. The authority bound is explicitly a function of state-estimation uncertainty, model uncertainty, and temporal delay, not merely a function of a safety monitor's binary verdict.

5.3 Relationship to Shielding

Shielding is a runtime enforcement method that ensures provable safety for reinforcement learning by blocking or correcting unsafe actions preemptively. A shield is a separate mechanism whose sole purpose is to reduce the amount of unsafe behavior the agent exhibits. Hazards are deliberately kept out of the reward since encoding them invites reward gaming; safety is instead enforced entirely by the shield, where it is a hard requirement rather than a soft reward term. PAC subsumes shielding as a special case: the shield implements the safety authority, and the shielded action space is \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}}.

The key difference is that PAC makes the shield's permissiveness a function of estimated uncertainty and available resources, whereas standard shields are typically computed offline from a fixed safety model.

