6. Hierarchical Feasibility and Mode Switching

6.1 The Infeasibility Problem

A CBF-based quadratic programming (CBF-QP) safety filter can easily become infeasible, potentially leading to violations or requiring a fallback strategy. Without proper knowledge of the control-invariant set, a wrongly chosen barrier function can lead to infeasibility of the CBF-QP and violation of safety. The naive formulation

\max_\pi \left[ J_{\mathrm{production}} - \lambda R \right]

allows sufficiently large production gains to compensate mathematically for increased risk. This is inappropriate for safety-critical systems.

6.2 Hierarchical Structure

The safety-first hierarchical framework decomposes the multi-objective problem into nested sub-problems in a safety-first approach. The proposed method guarantees solution feasibility while achieving improved performance, and it is scalable to an arbitrary number of CBF constraints. This scalability enables more precise and flexible representation of complex safety requirements using multiple simple CBFs. The proposed approach ensures that the formulated Hierarchical-CBF-QP is always feasible while strictly satisfying the top-prioritized safety-critical constraint.

PAC employs a three-level hierarchical structure:

Level 0 — Nominal mode. The safety constraint is hard and the performance objective is optimized within the safe set:

\max_\pi J_{\mathrm{performance}}(\pi) \quad \text{subject to} \quad R(\pi) \leq R_{\max}, \; x(t) \in \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{safe}} \; \forall t.

Level 1 — Recovery mode. When the safe set becomes empty or the QP is infeasible, the system switches to a least-violation controller that minimizes safety-margin degradation:

\min_{u} \| \max(0, -h(x)) \|.

Level 2 — Fail-safe mode. When recovery is impossible, the system transitions to an independently verified safe state.

6.3 Mode Switching Condition

Mode selection is triggered by emptiness of the safe set, not by its volume.

\text{Level 0} \iff \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) \neq \emptyset,

\text{Level 1} \iff \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset \;\wedge\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} \neq \emptyset,

\text{Level 2} \iff \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset \;\wedge\; \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} = \emptyset.

The continuous authority bound \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}} is active only in Level 0. In Levels 1 and 2 it is replaced by the recovery set and the fail-safe set respectively.

This preserves the continuity of authority within Level 0 (the AI's envelope shrinks smoothly as \Sigma, \tau, or resource depletion grows) while keeping the discrete switching logic of the Simplex/E-gas tradition. The AI's effective authority in Level 0 is

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) = \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) \cap \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}},

and the safety projection is

u_t = \Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}\!\left(u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}\right) = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{effective}}} \|u - u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}\|.

6.4 Feasibility Guarantee

Lemma 1 (Feasibility Guarantee). Let the hierarchical structure of Section 6.2 be in force, with \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}}, and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} as defined. Under the following assumptions:

(A1) The fail-safe action set \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} is non-empty and contains at least one action that drives the augmented state \tilde{x} = (x, r) toward \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} \times \mathcal{C} (or holds it at a verified safe state);

(A2) The resource state satisfies r_t \in \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} at the time of the query;

(A3) The safety authority has access to a certified approximation \hat{\mathcal{K}}_{\mathrm{res}} of the resource-aware viability kernel with \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}} \subseteq \hat{\mathcal{K}}_{\mathrm{res}} (i.e., the approximation is an over-approximation);

then for all t, there exists u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} such that the closed-loop remains defined and the hierarchical mode assignment of Section 6.3 is well-posed.

Proof. Three cases partition the state space.

Case 1: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) \neq \emptyset. Level 0 applies by definition, and any u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} yields a well-defined closed-loop trajectory by the forward-invariance property of the CBF condition (Section 2.3).

Case 2: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} \neq \emptyset. Level 1 applies. The recovery controller is defined as the minimizer of the safety-margin violation \min_u \|\max(0, -h(x))\|. By (A1) and (A2), the recovery set is non-empty, so the minimizer exists and is finite. The closed-loop remains defined because the recovery action is an element of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} by construction.

Case 3: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} = \emptyset. Level 2 applies. By (A1), the fail-safe action set is non-empty, so u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} exists. The fail-safe state is reachable by construction (this is the definition of fail-safe). ∎

Remark. The lemma does not assert that Level 0 is always feasible; it asserts that some level is always feasible. This is the correct guarantee for a hierarchical structure. The question "what if QP infeasible?" is answered by the mode-switching logic: infeasibility of Level 0 triggers Level 1, and infeasibility of Level 1 triggers Level 2. The only way the lemma fails is if (A1) fails—i.e., if no fail-safe action exists. This is a design requirement on the plant, not a theorem about the controller.

