7. Computational Realism: Certified Approximate Reachability

7.1 The Tractability Gap

Exact Hamilton–Jacobi reachability computes the maximal safe set by solving a partial differential equation over the state space. It scales exponentially in state dimension, making it intractable for any realistic CPS. Recent approaches to leveraging deep learning for computing reachable sets of continuous-time dynamical systems have gained popularity over traditional level-set methods, as they overcome the curse of dimensionality. However, considerable care needs to be taken in limiting approximation errors, particularly since no guarantees are provided during training on the accuracy of the learned reachable set.

7.2 Certified Approximate Reachability (CARe)

Certified Approximate Reachability (CARe) introduces an \epsilon-approximate Hamilton–Jacobi partial differential equation (HJ-PDE) that establishes a relationship between training loss and accuracy of the true reachable set. To formally certify this approximation, CARe leverages Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solvers to bound the residual error of the HJ-based loss function across the domain of interest. Leveraging Counter Example Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS), CARe closes the loop around learning and verification, by fine-tuning the neural network on counterexamples found by the SMT solver, thus improving the accuracy of the learned reachable set. To the best of our knowledge, CARe is the first approach to provide soundness guarantees on learned reachable sets of continuous dynamical systems.

7.3 Resource-Augmented Reachability

For the resource-augmented state \tilde{x} = (x, r), the reachable set computation is over a higher-dimensional space, which worsens tractability. Two mitigations:

1. Mixed-monotonicity decomposition. Where the resource dynamics are monotone in the actuation, mixed-monotonicity interval bounding can construct tight enclosures without solving the full HJ PDE in the augmented dimension.
2. Two-stage approximation. Compute the certified reachable set for x at fixed r, then propagate the resource dynamics separately. This loses the joint guarantee but is tractable and can be refined via CEGIS.

The framework should state explicitly that certified resource-aware reachability is an open problem and that the two-stage approximation is a stopgap.

7.4 Practical Guidance

For a given CPS, the choice of reachability method depends on:

· State dimension. Below n=6, exact level-set methods may be tractable. Above n=10, neural approximations are necessary.
· Available data. If the system is monotone, data-driven dominance functions can provide safety certificates from a small number of trajectories.
· Certification requirements. If formal guarantees are required, CARe-style methods with SMT-based error bounds are preferred.
· Real-time constraints. If the reachability computation must run online, neural approximations with pre-computed error bounds are the only viable option.

