8. Perception Interface and Uncertainty Quantification

8.1 The Problem

The framework treats \Sigma_t as if it were available. For learned perception modules, calibrated uncertainty is an open research problem. Deep neural networks are known to produce imperfect predictions, and the lack of methodologies for predicting neural network behavior has significantly impeded the broader application of machine learning in safety-critical systems.

8.2 Measurement-Robust Control Barrier Functions

The Measurement-Robust Control Barrier Function (MR-CBF) determines safe control inputs when facing measurement model uncertainty. MR-CBFs enforce safety in the presence of state estimates that suffer from bounded approximation error. This approximation error is represented as a function that can be learned from labeled data to capture the characteristics of the sensor and perception. Furthermore, MR-CBFs are used to inform sampling methodologies for learning-based perception systems and quantify tolerable error in the resulting learned models.

8.3 Conformal Robustness

Conformal robustness robustifies the Lyapunov and zeroing barrier certificates of data-driven dynamical systems against model prediction uncertainties using conformal prediction. It quantifies these uncertainties by leveraging rank statistics of prediction scores over system trajectories, without assuming any specific underlying structure of the prediction model or distribution of the uncertainties. With the quantified uncertainty information, one can construct the conformally robust control Lyapunov function (CR-CLF) and control barrier function (CR-CBF) for fully data-driven control with statistical guarantees of finite-horizon exponential stability and safety.

8.4 Minimal Perception Interface

A perception module \mathcal{P} is admissible for the safety authority if it provides one of the following three contracts:

(P1) Set-membership contract. \mathcal{P}(y_{0:t}) = \mathcal{X}_t \subseteq \mathcal{X} with the guarantee x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t. Set-membership state estimation computes sets of states consistent with a system model given bounded sets of disturbances and noise. Bounding the set of states is crucial for safety-critical applications so that one can ensure that all specifications are met.

(P2) Calibrated probabilistic contract. \mathcal{P}(y_{0:t}) = (\mathcal{X}_t, \delta_t) with \mathbb{P}(x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t) \geq 1 - \delta_t, where \delta_t is externally calibrated via conformal prediction or a known coverage gap.

(P3) Measurement-robust contract. \mathcal{P} provides a measurement function \hat{h}(x) with \|\hat{h}(x) - h(x)\| \leq \epsilon for all x \in \mathcal{X}. The safety authority replaces the CBF condition with an \epsilon-tightened version:

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[\nabla \hat{h}(x)^\top (f(x) + g(x)u)\right] \geq -\alpha(\hat{h}(x)) + L_h \epsilon,

where L_h is the Lipschitz constant of h. This is the MR-CBF construction of Dean et al.

Architectural principle. The safety authority interacts with perception only through one of (P1)–(P3). Any perception stack that cannot certify one of these contracts is outside the framework's scope, and the system must fall back to a non-learned estimator for the safety channel.

