9. Resource-Aware Viability Kernel

9.1 Definition

The resource-aware viability kernel is

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}} = \left\{ (x, r) : \exists \pi \; \forall d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}, \; (x(t), r(t)) \in \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} \; \forall t \geq 0 \right\}.

The safety-admissible action set is the projection

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \hat{r}, \Sigma) = \mathrm{proj}_u \left\{ (x, r, u) : (x, r) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}},\; \|x - \hat{x}\|_{\Sigma} \leq 1,\; u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} \right\}.

Monotonicity properties of both dynamics and constraints allow for analytical upper and lower approximations of the viability kernel through weakly and strongly invariant sets. The viability kernel is known to play a basic role for the analysis of such problems and the design of viable control feedbacks.

9.2 Consequences

1. Irreversibility becomes formal. An action that moves r_t outside \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} is excluded from \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} by construction.
2. The viability kernel is no longer static. It shrinks as r_t approaches \partial \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}}. This makes "authority as a dynamically computed quantity" operational rather than rhetorical.

