11. Mapping to Functional Safety Standards

11.1 The Standards Landscape

Existing standards such as IEC 61508 and ISO 26262 provide guidance for risk mitigation but lack explicit solutions for AI-driven systems. ISO/IEC TR 5469:2024 provides guidelines on ensuring the functional safety of AI systems. It addresses the unique risks associated with AI, such as unpredictability and lack of transparency, by recommending comprehensive risk assessment and mitigation strategies. The document describes properties, related risk factors, available methods and processes relating to the use of AI inside a safety-related function, the use of non-AI safety-related functions to ensure safety for AI-controlled equipment, and the use of AI systems to design and develop safety-related functions. ISO/PAS 8800 considers and adapts to road vehicles the general framework described in ISO/IEC TR 5469 on safety properties.

A modular reference architecture for safety-critical embodied AI systems aligns with functional safety and AI standards, including ISO/IEC TR 5469 and ISO/PAS 8800.

11.2 PAC Mapping

PAC Component Standards Mapping Precision
Safety Authority SEooC (Safety Element out of Context) Developed to ASIL D (ISO 26262) or SIL 3 (IEC 61508), independent from the AI module (which is QM / unrated)
Perception contracts (P1)–(P3) SOTIF (ISO 21448) + data validation per ISO/IEC TR 5469 Clause 6.3.2 (P1) set-membership = worst-case validation; (P2) conformal = probabilistic validation with coverage guarantee; (P3) MR-CBF = Lipschitz-bounded validation
Hierarchical feasibility E-gas monitoring concept (three-level) Level 0 = normal, Level 1 = recovery, Level 2 = fail-safe
Layer 1 enforcement Safety element out of context (SEooC) ASIL D / SIL 3 hardware interlocks
