12. Layered Assurance

12.1 The Regress Problem

The safety authority is itself a computational system. Who verifies it? Formal verification can establish properties of a mathematical model or abstraction, but it does not automatically prove that the physical plant exactly matches the model. A complete assurance chain requires

\boxed{\text{Model} \rightarrow \text{Formal Property} \rightarrow \text{Verification} \rightarrow \text{Model-to-Plant Assumptions} \rightarrow \text{Runtime Enforcement}.}

12.2 Layered Structure

PAC adopts a layered assurance approach:

Layer 1 — Formally verified inner layer. Hard actuator limits, emergency stops, and independently enforced physical constraints. This layer is simple enough to verify formally and is implemented in hardware or certified firmware.

Layer 2 — Runtime monitor layer. CBF/MPC-based safety filters, reachability computations, and feasibility checks. This layer is validated empirically through simulation and testing, with explicit error bounds from certified approximations.

Layer 3 — Governance layer. Human authority, operational procedures, and accountability structures. This layer provides accountability for residual risk that cannot be eliminated by Layers 1 and 2.

This does not eliminate the regress but makes it explicit and manageable. The assurance argument states clearly what is verified, what is tested, and what is assumed.

