14. Open Problems

14.1 Certified Resource-Aware Reachability

The augmented system \tilde{x} = (x, r) has higher dimension; the two-stage approximation loses the joint guarantee. This is a genuine open problem. CARe-style certification does not yet handle resource state directly.

14.2 Calibration Guarantees for (P2)

Conformal prediction provides finite-sample coverage under exchangeability, but exchangeability fails under distribution shift. The (P2) contract is only as strong as the calibration assumption, which must be stated.

14.3 Tightness of the MR-CBF Bound

The \epsilon-tightened CBF condition can be overly conservative. Recent work reduces conservatism by up to 68.5% compared to a measurement-robust CBF baseline while resolving deadlocks in narrow passages. The framework should cite this and treat the \epsilon-tightening as a baseline, not the state of the art.

14.4 Specification Gaming

The framework does not solve the specification gaming problem. An agent may satisfy its formal objective while violating the spirit of safety constraints. This requires governance-level solutions beyond the architectural mechanisms described here.

14.5 Scalable Certified Reachability

Certified approximate reachability provides formal error bounds, but the SMT-based certification is computationally expensive and may not scale to high-dimensional systems. Faster certification methods are needed for real-time deployment.

14.6 Full Proof of the Monotonicity Theorem

The monotonicity theorem for monotone systems requires a full proof establishing that the dominance function is a valid barrier certificate and that the superlevel sets are nested in the correct direction. The extension to resource-augmented states and uncertainty-dependent authority is new and requires verification.

