Appendix A: Control Barrier Functionals for Time-Delay Systems

A.1 Motivation

The Delayed Safety Proposition (Proposition 1) in Section 4.5 is stated for state delay (where the measurement is delayed but the actuation is instantaneous). For input delay (where the actuation itself is delayed), the reachable-set containment argument fails because the relative degree is affected by the delay itself. The correct machinery is the theory of control barrier functionals, which extends CBFs to systems with state delay by introducing functionals that enforce forward invariance of safe sets defined in the infinite-dimensional state space. The proposed framework is able to handle multiple delays and distributed delays both in the dynamics and in the safety condition, and provides an affine constraint on the control input that yields provable safety.

A.2 Functional Differential Equation Model

Consider the retarded functional differential equation (RFDE)

\dot{x}(t) = f(x_t) + g(x_t) u(t), \qquad x_t(\theta) := x(t+\theta), \quad \theta \in [-\tau, 0],

where x_t \in \mathcal{C}([-\tau, 0], \mathbb{R}^n) is the history segment. The safety condition is expressed as a functional

\mathcal{H}: \mathcal{C}([-\tau, 0], \mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathbb{R},

with safe set

\mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{del}} = \{ \phi \in \mathcal{C}([-\tau, 0], \mathbb{R}^n) : \mathcal{H}(\phi) \geq 0 \}.

A.3 Definition of Control Barrier Functionals

Definition A.1 (Control Barrier Functional). A continuously differentiable functional \mathcal{H}: \mathcal{C}([-\tau, 0], \mathbb{R}^n) \to \mathbb{R} is a control barrier functional (CBFal) for the RFDE if there exists a class-\mathcal{K} function \alpha such that for all \phi \in \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{del}},

\sup_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \left[ D\mathcal{H}(\phi; \dot{\phi}) \right] \geq -\alpha(\mathcal{H}(\phi)),

where D\mathcal{H}(\phi; \dot{\phi}) denotes the Lie derivative of \mathcal{H} along the RFDE trajectory.

A.4 Safety Theorem

Theorem A.1 (Forward Invariance via CBFals). Let \mathcal{H} be a control barrier functional for the RFDE, and let \phi_0 \in \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{del}}. Then any controller satisfying the CBFal condition

D\mathcal{H}(\phi; \dot{\phi}) \geq -\alpha(\mathcal{H}(\phi))

renders \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{del}} forward-invariant: \phi_t \in \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{del}} for all t \geq 0.

Proof. The proof follows the standard CBF argument extended to the infinite-dimensional state space. Suppose \mathcal{H}(\phi_0) \geq 0. The CBFal condition ensures that

\frac{d}{dt} \mathcal{H}(\phi_t) \geq -\alpha(\mathcal{H}(\phi_t))

along the closed-loop trajectory. By the comparison lemma, \mathcal{H}(\phi_t) \geq \beta(\mathcal{H}(\phi_0), t) for some class-\mathcal{KL} function \beta. Since \mathcal{H}(\phi_0) \geq 0 and \beta is non-negative for non-negative arguments, \mathcal{H}(\phi_t) \geq 0 for all t \geq 0. ∎

A.5 Affine Constraint for QP-Based Synthesis

The CBFal condition yields an affine constraint on the control input. If the functional takes the form

\mathcal{H}(\phi) = h(\phi(0)) + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \kappa(\theta) h(\phi(\theta)) \, d\theta,

for some kernel \kappa \geq 0, then the Lie derivative is

D\mathcal{H}(\phi; \dot{\phi}) = \nabla h(\phi(0))^\top (f(\phi) + g(\phi)u) + \int_{-\tau}^{0} \kappa(\theta) \nabla h(\phi(\theta))^\top (f(\phi_\theta) + g(\phi_\theta)u) \, d\theta.

This is affine in u, so the CBFal condition can be incorporated into a quadratic program:

u^* = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \frac{1}{2} \|u - u_{\mathrm{des}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad A(\phi) u \geq b(\phi),

where A(\phi) and b(\phi) are derived from the CBFal condition. The closed-loop system is an RFDE, and the safety guarantee holds for the infinite-dimensional state space.

A.6 Input-to-State Safe Control Barrier Functionals

For time-delay systems with disturbances, the notion of input-to-state safe control barrier functionals provides robustness guarantees. The system is modeled by nonlinear delay differential equations subject to disturbances, and the synthesis algorithm guarantees safety of the considered time-delay systems with disturbances.

