Appendix B: Feasibility Lemma and Hierarchical CBF-QP

B.1 The Infeasibility Problem

Control Barrier Function (CBF) based quadratic programs (QPs) have become an effective method for enforcing safety in safety-critical systems and robotics. However, these methods often suffer from infeasibility or overly conservative relaxations when handling multiple constraints, potentially compromising safety. The hierarchical CBF-QP framework, called "Safety-first," simultaneously incorporates performance objectives formulated using Control Lyapunov Functions (CLFs), and safety guarantees via CBFs with input constraints. Unlike existing approaches, the proposed method guarantees solution feasibility while achieving improved performance, and it is scalable to an arbitrary number of CBF constraints.

B.2 Hierarchical-CBF-QP Formulation

Consider k CBF constraints h_1, \ldots, h_k with a strict priority ordering h_1 \succ h_2 \succ \cdots \succ h_k. The Hierarchical-CBF-QP is formulated as a sequence of QPs:

Level 1:

u^{(1)} = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \frac{1}{2} \|u - u_{\mathrm{des}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f h_1 + L_g h_1 u + \alpha_1(h_1) \geq 0.

Level i (2 \leq i \leq k):

u^{(i)} = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \frac{1}{2} \|u - u^{(i-1)}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad L_f h_i + L_g h_i u + \alpha_i(h_i) \geq 0.

The top-prioritized safety-critical constraint is enforced as a hard constraint, while lower-priority constraints are enforced subject to the higher-priority ones being satisfied. The proposed approach ensures that the formulated Hierarchical-CBF-QP is always feasible while strictly satisfying the top-prioritized safety-critical constraint.

B.3 Feasibility Guarantee

Theorem B.1 (Feasibility of Hierarchical CBF-QP). Under the following assumptions:

(A1) The input constraint set \mathcal{U} is non-empty and convex;

(A2) The top-prioritized CBF h_1 has relative degree 1 and its gradient L_g h_1 \neq 0 on \partial \mathcal{C}_1;

(A3) The desired control u_{\mathrm{des}} is bounded;

the Hierarchical-CBF-QP admits a feasible solution at every time step.

Proof. The constraint for Level 1 is

L_f h_1(x) + L_g h_1(x) u + \alpha_1(h_1(x)) \geq 0.

This is a linear inequality in u. By (A2), the coefficient L_g h_1(x) \neq 0, so there exists u \in \mathcal{U} satisfying the inequality (take u sufficiently large in the direction of L_g h_1 if L_g h_1 > 0, or sufficiently negative if L_g h_1 < 0). Since \mathcal{U} is non-empty and convex (A1), the feasible set is non-empty. For Level i, the feasible set is the intersection of the feasible set of Level i-1 with the half-space defined by the i-th CBF constraint. If the intersection is empty, the QP at Level i is infeasible, but the solution from Level i-1 remains feasible for all higher-priority constraints. The hierarchical structure ensures that the top-prioritized constraint is always satisfied, and lower-priority constraints are enforced only when feasible. ∎

B.4 Feasibility Lemma (Restated)

Lemma 1 (Feasibility Guarantee of the Hierarchical Structure). Let the hierarchical structure of Section 6.2 be in force, with \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}, \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}}, and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} as defined. Under the following assumptions:

(A1) The fail-safe action set \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} is non-empty and contains at least one action that drives the augmented state \tilde{x} = (x, r) toward \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} \times \mathcal{C} (or holds it at a verified safe state);

(A2) The resource state satisfies r_t \in \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} at the time of the query;

(A3) The safety authority has access to a certified approximation \hat{\mathcal{K}}_{\mathrm{res}} of the resource-aware viability kernel with \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}} \subseteq \hat{\mathcal{K}}_{\mathrm{res}};

then for all t, there exists u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} such that the closed-loop remains defined and the hierarchical mode assignment of Section 6.3 is well-posed.

Proof. Three cases partition the state space.

Case 1: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \Sigma, \tau, r) \neq \emptyset. Level 0 applies by definition. Any u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} yields a well-defined closed-loop trajectory by the forward-invariance property of the CBF condition.

Case 2: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} \neq \emptyset. Level 1 applies. The recovery controller is the minimizer of \min_u \|\max(0, -h(x))\|. By (A1) and (A2), the recovery set is non-empty, so the minimizer exists and is finite. The closed-loop remains defined because the recovery action is an element of \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}}.

Case 3: \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} = \emptyset and \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{rec}} = \emptyset. Level 2 applies. By (A1), u_t \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{fail-safe}} exists. The fail-safe state is reachable by construction. ∎

B.5 Hierarchical Relaxation for Conflicting Safety Conditions

When CBF constraints are contradictory, the hierarchical relaxation approach addresses safety-critical control problems by ensuring that the formulated Hierarchical-CBF-QP is always feasible while strictly satisfying the top-prioritized safety-critical constraint. The method prioritizes critical constraints through hard enforcement while adaptively relaxing lower-priority constraints via slack variable optimization, ensuring feasibility under conflicting requirements.

