Appendix C: Resource-Aware Viability Kernel

C.1 Viability Kernel Definition

The invariance kernel of a set K \subseteq X under a dynamical system \mathcal{H} is the set of initial states \mathbf{x}_0 \in X for which all runs are viable in K. The invariance kernel is the largest closed invariance domain contained in K: a subset D \subset X is a viability invariance of F if \forall x \in D, F(x) \subset D.

The invariance kernel can be obtained by considering the sequence of subsets (K^n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}} with K^0 = K and

K^{n+1} := \{ x \in K^n \mid F(x) \subset K^n \text{ or } F(x) = \emptyset \}.

Write K_\omega := \bigcap_{n \in \mathbb{N}} K^n. If F is a lower-semicontinuous set-valued map and K is closed, then K_\omega = \operatorname{Inv}^{\mathcal{H}}(K).

C.2 Proof of the Invariance Kernel Characterization

Proof. We reason by double inclusion.

(\subseteq): Consider K_\omega. It contains any closed subset of K invariant under F: every set L \subseteq K which is invariant must be contained in \operatorname{Inv}^{\mathcal{H}}(K), since all runs starting in L stay in L and therefore in K.

(\supseteq): K_\omega is closed: K^0 = K is closed. If K^i is closed, since F is lower-semicontinuous, from the definition of K^{i+1} is closed. By induction, the K^i form a sequence of nested closed sets, and therefore K_\omega is closed (possibly the empty set). Consider a point x_0 \in \operatorname{Inv}^{\mathcal{H}}(K) and show that x_0 \in K_\omega. Assume, for the sake of contradiction, that x_0 \notin K_\omega. Then there exists N \geq 0 such that x \notin K^N. If N = 0, then x_0 \notin K^0 = K, therefore there exists a run starting at x_0 that is not viable in K. This contradicts the assumption that x_0 \in \operatorname{Inv}^{\mathcal{H}}(K). If N > 0, we show that there exists a run starting at x_0 that after at most one discrete transition finds itself outside K^{N-1}. The claim then follows by induction on N. Indeed, since x_0 \notin K^N, we must have F(x_0) \neq \emptyset and outside of K^{N-1}. But then there exists a run starting at x_0 that transitions outside K^{N-1} and we reach a contradiction as x_0 \notin K^{N-1}. Now, K_\omega is equal to \operatorname{Inv}^{\mathcal{H}}(K) as the latter is the largest closed invariance domain contained in K. ∎

C.3 Monotonicity Properties

Monotonicity properties of both dynamics and constraints allow for relevant analytical upper and lower approximations of the viability kernel through weakly and strongly invariant sets. The viability kernel is known to play a basic role for the analysis of such problems and the design of viable control feedbacks.

Theorem C.1 (Monotonicity of the Viability Kernel). Let f: X \times U \to X be a set-valued map and \kappa \subseteq X a constraint set. If f is monotone in the sense that x \leq x' implies f(x, u) \leq f(x', u), and \kappa is a down-set (i.e., x \in \kappa and x' \leq x implies x' \in \kappa), then the viability kernel \mathbb{V}(f, \kappa) is a down-set.

Proof. Let x \in \mathbb{V}(f, \kappa) and x' \leq x. We need to show that x' \in \mathbb{V}(f, \kappa). Since x \in \mathbb{V}(f, \kappa), there exists a control policy \pi such that all trajectories starting at x remain in \kappa. By monotonicity of f, for any control u, f(x', u) \leq f(x, u). By the down-set property of \kappa, if f(x, u) \in \kappa, then f(x', u) \in \kappa. Therefore, the same policy \pi keeps the trajectory starting at x' in \kappa. Hence x' \in \mathbb{V}(f, \kappa). ∎

C.4 Resource-Augmented Viability Kernel

For the resource-augmented state \tilde{x} = (x, r), the viability kernel is

\mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}} = \left\{ (x, r) : \exists \pi \; \forall d(\cdot) \in \mathcal{D}, \; (x(t), r(t)) \in \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} \; \forall t \geq 0 \right\}.

The safety-admissible action set is the projection

\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}}(\hat{x}, \hat{r}, \Sigma) = \mathrm{proj}_u \left\{ (x, r, u) : (x, r) \in \mathcal{K}_{\mathrm{res}},\; \|x - \hat{x}\|_{\Sigma} \leq 1,\; u \in \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{physical}} \right\}.

C.5 Consequences

1. Irreversibility becomes formal. An action that moves r_t outside \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}} is excluded from \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} by construction.
2. The viability kernel is no longer static. It shrinks as r_t approaches \partial \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{adm}}.

