Appendix E: Certified Approximate Reachability (CARe)

E.1 The Tractability Problem

Recent approaches to leveraging deep learning for computing reachable sets of continuous-time dynamical systems have gained popularity over traditional level-set methods, as they overcome the curse of dimensionality. However, considerable care needs to be taken in limiting approximation errors, particularly since no guarantees are provided during training on the accuracy of the learned reachable set. Traditional level-set methods suffer from the curse of dimensionality, as the number of required grid points grows exponentially with system dimensionality.

E.2 The CARe Framework

Certified Approximate Reachability (CARe) introduces an \epsilon-approximate Hamilton-Jacobi Partial Differential Equation (HJ-PDE), which establishes a relationship between training loss and accuracy of the true reachable set. To formally certify this approximation, CARe leverages Satisfiability Modulo Theories (SMT) solvers to bound the residual error of the HJ-based loss function across the domain of interest. Leveraging Counter Example Guided Inductive Synthesis (CEGIS), CARe closes the loop around learning and verification by fine-tuning the neural network on counterexamples found by the SMT solver, thus improving the accuracy of the learned reachable set.

E.3 Formal Guarantee

Theorem E.1 (CARe Soundness). Let \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{true}} be the true reachable set and \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{NN}} the neural network approximation. If the SMT solver certifies that the residual error of the HJ-based loss function is bounded by \epsilon across the domain of interest, then

\mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{NN}} \subseteq \mathcal{R}_{\mathrm{true}} \oplus \mathcal{B}_\epsilon,

where \mathcal{B}_\epsilon is the ball of radius \epsilon.

Proof sketch. The HJ-PDE characterizes the reachable set as the zero-level set of a value function V. The neural network approximates V by \hat{V}. The SMT solver bounds the residual |\mathcal{H}(\hat{V})| \leq \epsilon where \mathcal{H} is the HJ operator. By the comparison principle for viscosity solutions, the zero-level set of \hat{V} is within \epsilon of the zero-level set of V. ∎

E.4 Practical Considerations

Checking SMT queries with nonlinear real arithmetic can be computationally expensive, especially as unsatisfiability constitutes a full proof for validity. In practice, however, if a counterexample exists, the SMT solver can often find it quickly. The CEGIS loop iteratively refines the neural network until the SMT solver can certify the bound.

E.5 Resource-Augmented Reachability

For the resource-augmented state \tilde{x} = (x, r), the reachable set computation is over a higher-dimensional space, which worsens tractability. Two mitigations:

1. Mixed-monotonicity decomposition. Where the resource dynamics are monotone in the actuation, mixed-monotonicity interval bounding can construct tight enclosures without solving the full HJ PDE in the augmented dimension.
2. Two-stage approximation. Compute the certified reachable set for x at fixed r, then propagate the resource dynamics separately. This loses the joint guarantee but is tractable and can be refined via CEGIS.

The framework states explicitly that certified resource-aware reachability is an open problem and that the two-stage approximation is a stopgap.

