I.2 Glossary of Terms

Authority gap: The difference between what an AI agent proposes (u_t^{\mathrm{AI}}) and what is actually applied (u_t).

Control Barrier Function (CBF): A function h whose superlevel set is forward-invariant under a control law satisfying the CBF condition.

Control Barrier Functional (CBFal): The extension of CBFs to time-delay systems, defined on the infinite-dimensional state space of history segments.

Dominance function: A dissipative, monotone function constructed from trajectory data that serves as a safety certificate for monotone systems.

Feasibility Lemma: The guarantee that the hierarchical structure admits a feasible action at every time step.

Measurement-Robust CBF (MR-CBF): A CBF that accounts for bounded measurement error in the state estimate.

Resource-aware viability kernel: The set of augmented states from which safety can be maintained indefinitely while respecting resource constraints.

Safety authority: The architectural component that computes \mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{safe}} and enforces \Pi_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Safety-first hierarchical CBF-QP: A sequential QP formulation that prioritizes safety constraints and guarantees feasibility.

