Appendix K: Open Problems

1. Full proof of the Monotonicity Theorem. The proof for monotone systems requires establishing that the dominance function is a valid barrier certificate and that the superlevel sets are nested in the correct direction.
2. Certified resource-aware reachability. The augmented system \tilde{x} = (x, r) has higher dimension; the two-stage approximation loses the joint guarantee.
3. Calibration guarantees for (P2). Conformal prediction provides finite-sample coverage under exchangeability, but exchangeability fails under distribution shift.
4. Tightness of the MR-CBF bound. The \epsilon-tightened CBF condition can be overly conservative.
5. Specification gaming. The framework does not solve the specification gaming problem.
6. Scalable certified reachability. The SMT-based certification is computationally expensive and may not scale to high-dimensional systems.
7. Input delay. The Delayed Safety Proposition assumes state delay; input delay requires the control barrier functional machinery.
8. Full proof of the Feasibility Lemma for the general case. The proof assumes (A1)–(A3); the general case where the fail-safe set is empty requires additional analysis.



